  "query_preview": "..An upper low was currently centered over northern \nArizona, moving slowly to the northeast. Drier air has moved in \nbehind this system across southeast Arizona, with satellite layer \nblended PW values in the 0.25-0.40 inch range east of Tucson and \nbetween 0.50-0.60 inches west of Tucson. Scattered clouds across \nportions of southeast Arizona will shift north and east of the \nforecast area during the mid to late morning hours today.   \n\nHigh pressure aloft will build across the desert southwest behind \nthe upper low late today through Wednesday. This will result in dry \nand warmer conditions across southeast Arizona. \n\nLate this week into next weekend the models show an active pattern, \nwith west coast troughing and southern stream moisture moving into \nthe southern half of the west coast and across the Intermountain \nWest. There continues to be timing/track differences, with the GFS \nfocusing more on the northern half of Arizona into the Great Basin, \nwhile the ECMWF is focused further south across the desert \nsouthwest. Either way, the weather turns unsettled late in the day \nThursday, with increasing chances for showers/thunderstorms Friday \nand Saturday across southeast Arizona and then the potential for \nsubtropical moisture plume influence late Saturday and Sunday.   \n\nOverall, high temperatures will be 2-4 degrees below normal today, \nwarming to near to slightly above normal Monday through Thursday, \nthen lowering to 8-10 degrees below normal next weekend.",
